<p>The sections below show the less obvious rows as programs.</p>

<h2 id="values">Values, lists and maps</h2>
<p>A Bayan program is a set of typed values. You can leave the type off a local when the right-hand side shows it (<code>let name = "Ada"</code>), and you write it for collections that start empty (<code>List&lt;i64&gt;()</code>). Blocks use braces, not indentation, and a statement ends at the end of the line.</p>
<pre><code class="other" data-lang="Python">scores = [90, 72, 85]
total = 0
for s in scores:
    total += s
print(f"{len(scores)} scores, total {total}, mean {total / len(scores)}")
ages = {"ada": 36, "grace": 45}
print("ada" in ages, "linus" in ages)
for name, age in ages.items():
    print(name, "is", age)</code></pre>
<example name="guide-py-basics"/>
<p>The mean is <code>82</code>, not <code>82.33</code>. Dividing two integers gives an integer, rounded toward zero. Python's <code>/</code> always gives a float, and Bayan has no <code>//</code>. To get a fraction, convert first: <code>f64(total) / f64(scores.length)</code>.</p>
<p>A <code>Map</code> keeps the order in which keys were first inserted, like a Python <code>dict</code>. Reading a key that is not there stops the program (a panic), the way <code>d["x"]</code> raises <code>KeyError</code>. When the key may be missing, use <code>tryGet(at:)</code>, which gives a value that may be <code>null</code>, and finish with <code>??</code>.</p>

<h2 id="structs">Classes become structs</h2>
<p>Bayan has no classes and no inheritance. Data lives in structs, and a struct is a value. A struct can have default field values, properties (a name with <code>=&gt;</code> and no parentheses) and methods.</p>
<pre><code class="other" data-lang="Python">@dataclass
class Account:
    owner: str
    balance: int = 0

    @property
    def is_empty(self):
        return self.balance == 0

    def deposit(self, amount):
        self.balance += amount

a = Account("ada")
a.deposit(50)
b = replace(a, owner="grace")
a.deposit(25)</code></pre>
<example name="guide-py-class"/>
<p>Two things differ from the Python version.</p>
<ul>
<li>A method that changes the struct says <code>ref this</code> as its first parameter. Without it, the method can only read. You cannot call a changing method on a <code>let</code>.</li>
<li><code>b</code> is a separate value. <code>a with { owner: "grace" }</code> copies <code>a</code> and replaces one field, so the later <code>a.deposit(25)</code> does not touch <code>b</code>. In Python two names can point at one object. In Bayan they cannot, unless you ask for a <code>ref</code>.</li>
</ul>

<h2 id="none">None becomes an optional</h2>
<p>A value that may be missing has a type that ends in <code>?</code>. The compiler does not let you use it as if it were there, so there is no <code>AttributeError: 'NoneType'</code>. Test it with <code>is not null</code>, which also narrows the type inside the block, or give a fallback with <code>??</code>.</p>
<pre><code class="other" data-lang="Python">def find(names, prefix):
    for n in names:
        if n.startswith(prefix):
            return n
    return None</code></pre>
<example name="guide-py-none"/>
<p>The second parameter of <code>find</code> is written <code>by string prefix</code>. When a function takes more than one required parameter, all but the first carry a label, called a particle, and the call writes it: <code>find(names, by: "gr")</code>. The <a href="parameters.html">parameters page</a> lists the particles and when to pick which.</p>
<note>Planned: <code>?.</code>, as in <code>name?.length</code>. The parser rejects it today (BYN2022). Use <code>is not null</code> or <code>??</code>.</note>

<h2 id="errors">Exceptions become fails and catch</h2>
<p>Nothing is raised in the dark. A function that can fail lists the error type after its parameters, with <code>fails</code>. The caller must either pass the failure up with <code>try</code> or handle it with <code>catch</code>. Python's <code>except</code> clauses map to the arms of <code>catch switch</code>, and the compiler checks that you covered every case.</p>
<pre><code class="other" data-lang="Python">class ParseError(Exception): pass

def parse(text):
    if not text:
        raise ParseError("empty")
    return int(text)

try:
    n = parse("")
except ParseError:
    n = 0</code></pre>
<example name="guide-py-errors"/>
<p>The three calls cover the three shapes. <code>catch -1</code> gives a fallback for any failure. <code>catch switch</code> picks a value per case. The last one failed with <code>.notNumber</code> and used <code>-2</code>. The <a href="errors.html">Errors guide</a> goes through the rest, including errors that carry data.</p>

<h2 id="files">Files</h2>
<p>There is no <code>with</code> block. <code>Io.readText</code> reads the whole file and closes it before it returns, so there is nothing to release. Every call that touches the outside world can fail with <code>IoError</code>, so the function that makes the call declares it, or the call is wrapped in <code>catch</code>.</p>
<pre><code class="other" data-lang="Python">with open(path, "w") as f:
    f.write("one\ntwo\nthree\n")
with open(path) as f:
    for n, line in enumerate(f, 1):
        print(f"{n}: {line.rstrip()}")</code></pre>
<example name="guide-py-files"/>
<p>For cleanup that is not a single call, <code>defer</code> runs a statement when the scope ends. It is the closest thing to a <code>with</code> block or a <code>finally</code>. See <a href="memory.html#defer">Memory and ownership</a>.</p>

<h2 id="lists">Comprehensions, sorting and lambdas</h2>
<p>There are no comprehensions yet. You accumulate in a loop. <code>sort()</code> sorts in place, so to keep the original you copy first. A copy is always written out (<code>xs.copy()</code>), because lists own their memory.</p>
<pre><code class="other" data-lang="Python">xs = [3, 1, 2]
ys = sorted(xs)
squares = [x * x for x in xs]</code></pre>
<example name="guide-py-sort"/>
<note>Planned: <code>where</code>, <code>select</code> and <code>sumBy</code> on lists, which take a lambda written with <code>it</code>. They are in the specification but not in the compiler yet (<code>xs.where(it &gt; 1)</code> gives BYN2003 today).</note>
<p>A lambda needs a function type to be checked against, so it appears where a function is expected: <code>apply(5, by: it * 3)</code> or <code>apply(5, by: x =&gt; x - 1)</code>. You cannot write <code>let f = x =&gt; x + 1</code> on its own line.</p>

<h2 id="match">match becomes switch</h2>
<p>A union lists the shapes a value can take. A <code>switch</code> over it must cover every case, and a case can bind the fields it carries. This replaces both a class hierarchy and <code>isinstance</code> chains.</p>
<pre><code class="other" data-lang="Python">match shape:
    case Circle(r): return 3.14 * r * r
    case Rect(w, h): return w * h
    case Point(): return 0.0</code></pre>
<example name="guide-py-match"/>
<p>Add a case to <code>Shape</code> and every <code>switch</code> that forgot it stops compiling. That is the point. The leading dot in <code>.circle(let r)</code> means "the case of the type the compiler expects here". The whole numbers print as <code>6</code> and <code>0</code>, since a float with no fraction prints without a decimal point.</p>

<h2 id="surprises">What will surprise you</h2>

<h3 id="s-types">Everything has a type, and integers have a size</h3>
<p><code>i64</code> is a 64-bit integer, <code>i32</code> a 32-bit one, <code>u8</code> a byte. Python's <code>int</code> never overflows. Bayan's do, and an overflow stops the program with a panic instead of wrapping around or growing:</p>
<figure class="diag">
<pre><span class="err">panic:</span> arithmetic overflow
  at overflow.byn:3</pre>
<figcaption>Real output from the v0.1 compiler. The path is shortened.</figcaption>
</figure>
<p>A smaller integer converts to a wider one on its own, but never the other way. You write <code>i32(a)</code> and you decide that the value fits.</p>
<figure class="diag">
<pre><span class="err">error BYN2202:</span> expected `i32`, found `i64`
  --&gt; narrow.byn:3:18

  3 │     let b: i32 = a
    │                  ^
    │
  help: convert it if the value always fits; `i64` does not convert to `i32` without loss
    │ 3 │     let b: i32 = i32(a)
    │   │                  ++++++

✗ build failed: 1 error, 0 warnings
  explain: bayan explain BYN2202</pre>
<figcaption>Real output from the v0.1 compiler. The path is shortened.</figcaption>
</figure>
<p><b>Why:</b> a silent wraparound or a silent loss of bits is a bug that shows up far from its cause. Bayan makes the narrowing visible and the overflow loud.</p>

<h3 id="s-truth">There is no truthiness</h3>
<p>A condition is a <code>bool</code>. Empty lists, zero and <code>null</code> are not false. Write the test you mean: <code>xs.isEmpty</code>, <code>n != 0</code>, <code>x is null</code>.</p>
<figure class="diag">
<pre><span class="err">error BYN2202:</span> expected `bool`, found `i32`
  --&gt; truthy.byn:3:8

  3 │     if n {
    │        ^

✗ build failed: 1 error, 0 warnings
  explain: bayan explain BYN2202</pre>
<figcaption>Real output from the v0.1 compiler. The path is shortened.</figcaption>
</figure>
<p><b>Why:</b> <code>if items:</code> cannot tell "no items" from "items is None". Spelling the test removes the guess.</p>

<h3 id="s-copy">Assignment does not alias</h3>
<p>In Python <code>b = a</code> makes two names for one list. A Bayan <code>List</code> owns its memory, so there is no such thing as two names for one. Writing <code>var b = a</code> is an error, and the compiler offers the options: <code>move a</code> to hand the list over (then <code>a</code> is gone), <code>a.copy()</code> for an independent copy, or pass it by <code>ref</code> to share it.</p>
<figure class="diag">
<pre><span class="err">error BYN2780:</span> implicit copy of a unique value: `List&lt;i64&gt;` is unique: it owns its storage
  --&gt; copy.byn:3:13

  3 │     var b = a
    │             ^
    │
  help: hand the value over with `move a`; make an independent copy with `a.copy(to: alloc)`; pass
        it by `ref` to share it instead
    │ 3 │     var b = move a
    │   │             +++++

✗ build failed: 1 error, 0 warnings
  explain: bayan explain BYN2780</pre>
<figcaption>Real output from the v0.1 compiler. The path is shortened.</figcaption>
</figure>
<p>Plain values such as numbers and <code>bool</code> copy freely. Only types that own storage, such as lists and maps, must be moved or copied by name.</p>
<p><b>Why:</b> a copy of a big list costs time and memory, and a shared one can change under you. Bayan makes you choose and shows the choice in the source. See <a href="memory.html">Memory and ownership</a>.</p>

<h3 id="s-let">let is the default</h3>
<p>A <code>let</code> cannot be assigned twice, and a method that changes a struct cannot be called on one.</p>
<figure class="diag">
<pre><span class="err">error BYN2104:</span> `x` is immutable
  --&gt; immut.byn:3:5

  3 │     x = 2
    │     ^ declared with `let` on line 2
    │
  help: declare it with `var` if it should change
    │ 2 │     var x = 1
    │   │     +++

✗ build failed: 1 error, 0 warnings
  explain: bayan explain BYN2104</pre>
<figcaption>Real output from the v0.1 compiler. The path is shortened.</figcaption>
</figure>
<p>Declare <code>var</code> where a value really changes, and the compiler warns when a <code>var</code> is never reassigned. <b>Why:</b> when you read a function, every <code>let</code> is a fact you do not need to track.</p>

<h3 id="s-strings">Strings are bytes you view, not a sequence you rebuild</h3>
<p>A <code>string</code> is immutable UTF-8 text. <code>length</code> counts bytes and <code>runeCount</code> counts characters, so <code>"héllo"</code> has length 6 and <code>runeCount</code> 5. You slice with byte offsets, <code>s[0..5]</code> (end excluded), and a cut that splits a character panics. There is no <code>+</code> on strings:</p>
<figure class="diag">
<pre><span class="err">error BYN2209:</span> strings cannot be joined with `+`; use interpolation
  --&gt; concat.byn:3:11

  3 │     print(a + "y")
    │           ^^^^^^^
    │
  help: write the parts as one interpolation: `$"{a}{b}"`, or `buf.append($"{a}{b}")` in a loop
    │ 3 │     print($"{a}y")
    │   │           +++++

✗ build failed: 1 error, 0 warnings
  explain: bayan explain BYN2209</pre>
<figcaption>Real output from the v0.1 compiler. The path is shortened.</figcaption>
</figure>
<p>Use interpolation, <code>$"{a}y"</code>, or a <code>StrBuf</code> in a loop. <b>Why:</b> repeated <code>+</code> copies the text each time, and the cost hides in a line that looks free.</p>

<h3 id="s-params">Arguments carry labels</h3>
<p>Python lets you pass any argument by keyword. Bayan requires a label, a particle, on every required argument after the first, and the particle comes from a fixed list (<code>from</code>, <code>to</code>, <code>until</code>, <code>through</code>, <code>at</code>, <code>by</code>, <code>for</code>, <code>as</code>). A call like <code>find(names, by: "gr")</code> says what the second argument is for. <b>Why:</b> a call site reads as a sentence, and swapped arguments become a compile error. See <a href="parameters.html">Parameters and arguments</a>.</p>

<h3 id="s-no">What is not there</h3>
<ul>
<li>No classes, inheritance, decorators, generators or <code>yield</code>, and no <code>None</code> in a type that does not end in <code>?</code>.</li>
<li>No exceptions. A panic (index out of range, overflow, division by zero) is a bug report, not something you catch.</li>
<li>No <code>import</code> at the top of a script for the basics. <code>Io</code>, <code>List</code>, <code>Map</code> and <code>print</code> are available as they are. A directory is a module.</li>
<li>No dynamic features: no <code>getattr</code>, no <code>eval</code>, no adding fields at run time.</li>
</ul>
<p>Next: <a href="reading.html">Reading Bayan code</a> explains the forms you will see in other people's programs, and <a href="effective.html">Effective Bayan</a> says which form to choose.</p>
